  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Is the sum of two injective functions from $\\mathbb{R}$ to $\\mathbb{R}$ necessarily injective? Either prove this or give a counterexample.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No. For example, $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=-x$ are injective, but $(f+g)(x)=0$ is not injective.",
      "worked": "No. Let $f,g:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be defined by $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=-x$. If $f(a)=f(b)$, then $a=b$. If $g(a)=g(b)$, then $-a=-b$, so $a=b$. Thus both functions are injective.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(f+g)(x)&=x+(-x)\\\\(f+g)(x)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIn particular, $(f+g)(0)=(f+g)(1)=0$, although $0\\ne1$. Therefore, their sum is not injective.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
